&lt;h1 class="relative group">Exploring HTML Containers - Part 1: Introduction to HTML Containers
&lt;div id="exploring-html-containers---part-1-introduction-to-html-containers" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#exploring-html-containers---part-1-introduction-to-html-containers" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>A container wraps other elements. These seven are the ones pages use most.&lt;/p>
&lt;table>
&lt;thead>
&lt;tr>
&lt;th>Tag&lt;/th>
&lt;th>Block or inline&lt;/th>
&lt;th>When to use it&lt;/th>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/thead>
&lt;tbody>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>div&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>Group elements for layout. It has no meaning of its own.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>span&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>inline&lt;/td>
&lt;td>Style a few words inside a line.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>section&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>A topic that has its own heading.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>article&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>A piece that could stand alone.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>main&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>The one main content area of the page.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>header&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>The top of a page or of a section.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;tr>
&lt;td>&lt;code>footer&lt;/code>&lt;/td>
&lt;td>block&lt;/td>
&lt;td>The close of a page or of a section.&lt;/td>
&lt;/tr>
&lt;/tbody>
&lt;/table>
&lt;h2 class="relative group">What are different containers in HTML?
&lt;div id="what-are-different-containers-in-html" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#what-are-different-containers-in-html" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>In HTML and CSS, a &lt;strong>&amp;ldquo;container&amp;rdquo; tag&lt;/strong> isn’t a special keyword — it’s a &lt;strong>general term&lt;/strong> used to describe any element that wraps or groups other elements. They don&amp;rsquo;t of any size of their own, but the size of all the elements put inside the container is their size.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>